1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,491
Welcome to Jerry Documentaries.

2
00:00:02,491 --> 00:00:13,800
In this first episode we travel to the largest hot desert on Earth, the Sahara, and we discover that it is very different from the pictures in our minds.

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:22,028
The Sahara covers about nine million square kilometers, an area close to the size of the United States.

4
00:00:22,028 --> 00:00:29,400
It stretches across North Africa, from the Atlantic Ocean in the west to the Red Sea in the east.

5
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:35,930
Many people imagine endless sand, but sand dunes cover only a part of the Sahara.

6
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:43,800
Most of it is bare rock plateaus and gravel plains, and there are even high mountains inside the desert.

7
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,071
The weather is extreme.

8
00:00:46,071 --> 00:00:52,092
Days can be extremely hot, and because the air is dry, nights can be cold.

9
00:00:52,092 --> 00:00:58,600
Rain is rare in many areas, and some places can go years without a single storm.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,744
But the Sahara was not always a desert.

11
00:01:01,744 --> 00:01:09,905
Several thousand years ago, a wetter period turned large parts of it into grasslands with lakes, rivers and animals.

12
00:01:09,905 --> 00:01:13,000
Scientists call it the African Humid Period.

13
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:15,294
We know this from rock art.

14
00:01:15,294 --> 00:01:22,869
In places like Tassili n'Ajjer in Algeria, ancient people painted cattle, giraffes and hunters on the stone.

15
00:01:22,869 --> 00:01:28,200
These images are thousands of years old and show a very different landscape.

16
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:31,508
Later, the desert became a road for trade.

17
00:01:31,508 --> 00:01:43,000
Caravans of camels carried salt, gold and other goods between North Africa and West Africa, and trading cities such as Timbuktu became centers of wealth and learning.

18
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:46,156
Life in the Sahara gathers around water.

19
00:01:46,156 --> 00:01:50,083
In oases, underground water feeds date palms and gardens.

20
00:01:50,083 --> 00:01:57,800
Communities such as the Amazigh and the Tuareg have lived here for centuries, with deep knowledge of the desert.

21
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:00,854
Even the Sahara's dust has a story.

22
00:02:00,854 --> 00:02:10,939
Winds lift fine dust and carry it across the Atlantic Ocean, where it can reach the Amazon rainforest and bring minerals to its soil.

23
00:02:10,939 --> 00:02:14,200
Our planet is more connected than we think.

24
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:16,200
Thank you for watching.

25
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:21,835
In the next episode we leave the desert and follow a message across the internet.

26
00:02:21,835 --> 00:02:25,800
Follow Jerry Development for the full documentary series.

